\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



For a prime $p$, write $\mathcal P_p=\{q<p:q\text{ is prime}\}$. The question is whether $\mathcal P_p\mathcal P_p=({\mathbb{Z}}/p{\mathbb{Z}})^\times$ for every sufficiently large $p$, where products are reduced modulo $p$ and the same prime may be used twice. The threshold must be independent of the requested nonzero residue. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.07679}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $p$ be a large prime, and let $A$ be the set of all primes less than $p$. Is every $x \in \{1, \dots, p-1\}$ congruent to some product $a_1a_2$ modulo $p$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Show that all p-1 nonzero classes modulo every sufficiently large prime p are represented by two primes below p, or find an obstruction. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every sufficiently large prime modulus, can every nonzero residue be represented by the product of two smaller primes?

